{"date":"2026-09-13","post_id":1318856,"title":"Comet NEOWISE Rising Over the Adriatic Sea","permalink":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-september-13-comet-neowise-rising-over-the-adriatic-sea/","media_type":"video","explanation":"<strong>Explanation:\u00a0</strong>This sight was worth getting out of bed early. Just over four years ago,\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C/2020_F3_(NEOWISE)\">Comet C/2020 F3 (NEOWISE)</a>\u00a0rose before dawn to the\u00a0<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/brown-tabby-cat-7GX5aICb5i4\">delight</a>\u00a0of northern sky enthusiasts awake that early. Up before sunrise on July 8th, the featured photographer was able to capture in dramatic fashion one of the few comets\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/media/set/?vanity=APOD.Sky&amp;set=a.2762832093820992\">visible</a>\u00a0to the unaided eye this century, an inner-<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/\">Solar System</a>\u00a0intruder that has become known as the\u00a0<a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap131013.html\">Great Comet</a>\u00a0of 2020. The\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/CCXz9yUIOQb/\">resulting video</a>\u00a0detailed Comet NEOWISE from\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy\">Italy</a>\u00a0rising over the\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adriatic_Sea\">Adriatic Sea</a>. The\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/ts0Ek3nLHew\">featured time-lapse video</a>\u00a0combines over 240 images taken over 30 minutes.\u00a0<a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap200707.html\">The comet</a>\u00a0was seen rising through a foreground of bright and undulating\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noctilucent_cloud\">noctilucent clouds</a>, and before a background of distant stars.\u00a0<a href=\"https://earthsky.org/space/how-to-see-comet-c2020-f3-neowise\">Comet NEOWISE</a>\u00a0remained unexpectedly bright for over a month, with its\u00a0<a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap210308.html\">ion and dust</a>\u00a0tails\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/feature/jpl/comet-neowise-sizzles-as-it-slides-by-the-sun-providing-a-treat-for-observers\">found to emanate</a>\u00a0from a\u00a0<a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap100104.html\">nucleus</a>\u00a0spanning about five kilometers across.<br><br><strong>APOD's main NASA site is\u00a0<a href=\"https://asterisk.apod.com/viewtopic.php?t=45023\">moving</a>:</strong>\u00a0From\u00a0<a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/\">apod.nasa.gov</a>\u00a0to\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod\">science.nasa.gov/apod</a><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:\u00a0</strong>open space","credit":"<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/astrogyres/\">Paolo Girotti</a>","copyright":"<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/astrogyres/\">Paolo Girotti</a>","alt":"Comet in the sky low to the horizon above the clouds.","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-september-13-comet-neowise-rising-over-the-adriatic-sea/","hdurl":"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/Comet_snapshot.png?w=1080&h=1350&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint","basic_html":"<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 September 13 - Comet NEOWISE Rising Over the Adriatic Sea</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-september-13-comet-neowise-rising-over-the-adriatic-sea/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation:\u00a0This sight was worth getting out of bed early. Just over four years ago,\u00a0Comet C/2020 F3 (NEOWISE)\u00a0rose before dawn to the\u00a0delight\u00a0of northern sky enthusiasts awake that early. Up before sunrise on July 8th, the featured photographer was able to capture in dramatic fashion one of the few comets\u00a0visible\u00a0to the unaided eye this century, an inner-Solar System\u00a0intruder that has become known as the\u00a0Great Comet\u00a0of 2020. The\u00a0resulting video\u00a0detailed Comet NEOWISE from\u00a0Italy\u00a0rising over the\u00a0Adriatic Sea. The\u00a0featured time-lapse video\u00a0combines over 240 images taken over 30 minutes.\u00a0The comet\u00a0was seen rising through a foreground of bright and undulating\u00a0noctilucent clouds, and before a background of distant stars.\u00a0Comet NEOWISE\u00a0remained unexpectedly bright for over a month, with its\u00a0ion and dust\u00a0tails\u00a0found to emanate\u00a0from a\u00a0nucleus\u00a0spanning about five kilometers across.APOD&#039;s main NASA site is\u00a0moving:\u00a0From\u00a0apod.nasa.gov\u00a0to\u00a0science.nasa.gov/apodTomorrow&#039;s picture:\u00a0open space\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"comet neowise rising over the adriatic sea\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Comet NEOWISE Rising Over the Adriatic Sea\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-september-13-comet-neowise-rising-over-the-adriatic-sea/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/Comet_snapshot.png?w=1080&#038;h=1350&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/Comet_snapshot.png?w=1080&#038;h=1350&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"Comet in the sky low to the horizon above the clouds.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-09-13T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-09-11T17:18:17-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Comet NEOWISE Rising Over the Adriatic Sea\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/Comet_snapshot.png?w=1080&#038;h=1350&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 September 13<br>\n\n<video controls title=\"\">\n<source src=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/NoctilucentNeowise_Girotti.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\">\n</video>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Comet NEOWISE Rising Over the Adriatic Sea </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/astrogyres/\">Paolo Girotti</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:\u00a0</strong>This sight was worth getting out of bed early. Just over four years ago,\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C/2020_F3_(NEOWISE)\">Comet C/2020 F3 (NEOWISE)</a>\u00a0rose before dawn to the\u00a0<a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/brown-tabby-cat-7GX5aICb5i4\">delight</a>\u00a0of northern sky enthusiasts awake that early. Up before sunrise on July 8th, the featured photographer was able to capture in dramatic fashion one of the few comets\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/media/set/?vanity=APOD.Sky&amp;set=a.2762832093820992\">visible</a>\u00a0to the unaided eye this century, an inner-<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/\">Solar System</a>\u00a0intruder that has become known as the\u00a0<a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap131013.html\">Great Comet</a>\u00a0of 2020. The\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/CCXz9yUIOQb/\">resulting video</a>\u00a0detailed Comet NEOWISE from\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy\">Italy</a>\u00a0rising over the\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adriatic_Sea\">Adriatic Sea</a>. The\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/ts0Ek3nLHew\">featured time-lapse video</a>\u00a0combines over 240 images taken over 30 minutes.\u00a0<a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap200707.html\">The comet</a>\u00a0was seen rising through a foreground of bright and undulating\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noctilucent_cloud\">noctilucent clouds</a>, and before a background of distant stars.\u00a0<a href=\"https://earthsky.org/space/how-to-see-comet-c2020-f3-neowise\">Comet NEOWISE</a>\u00a0remained unexpectedly bright for over a month, with its\u00a0<a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap210308.html\">ion and dust</a>\u00a0tails\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/feature/jpl/comet-neowise-sizzles-as-it-slides-by-the-sun-providing-a-treat-for-observers\">found to emanate</a>\u00a0from a\u00a0<a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap100104.html\">nucleus</a>\u00a0spanning about five kilometers across.<br><br><strong>APOD's main NASA site is\u00a0<a href=\"https://asterisk.apod.com/viewtopic.php?t=45023\">moving</a>:</strong>\u00a0From\u00a0<a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/\">apod.nasa.gov</a>\u00a0to\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod\">science.nasa.gov/apod</a><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:\u00a0</strong>open space\n<p> <center>\n\n<b> Tomorrow's picture: </b><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-september-14-where-your-elements-came-from/\">APOD: 2026 September 14 &#8211; Where Your Elements Came From</a>\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-september-12-apollo-11-catching-some-sun/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-september-14-where-your-elements-came-from/\">&gt;</a>\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>","basic_html_url":"https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/260913/html"}